{{- $http := uses "http"}}{{$port := 8080}}{{with optionsOf "http"}}{{$port = .port}}{{end -}}
# {{.App.Label}}

Generated by [esquadro](https://github.com/patrickluzdev/esquadro) from the
`.esq` files in `esquadro/`: you describe the modules and their entities
there, and esquadro writes the Go code, the database migrations{{if uses "rest"}} and the
API{{end}}. This README is yours: esquadro wrote it once and never changes it.

## Getting started

You need [Go](https://go.dev/dl) and [Task](https://taskfile.dev){{if uses "compose"}}, and
[Docker](https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/) for the local services{{end}}.

```
{{- if uses "compose"}}
task up        # start the local services (compose.yaml): {{if uses "postgres"}}Postgres{{end}}
{{- end}}
{{- if uses "migrations"}}
task migrate   # create the tables
{{- end}}
task run       # run it{{if $http}}: http://localhost:{{$port}}{{end}}
```
{{- if uses "api_docs"}}

The API's documentation is at http://localhost:{{$port}}/docs, where every
operation can be tried, and its contract at `api/openapi.yaml`.
{{- end}}

```
task test      # run the tests{{if uses "testcontainers"}} (they start their own Postgres: Docker){{end}}
task lint      # gofmt and go vet
{{- if uses "compose"}}
task down      # stop the local services
{{- end}}
```

## Changing the model

1. Edit the `.esq` files in `esquadro/`: add a field, an entity, a module
   (one file per module, starting with `package <module>`).
2. Run `esquadro gen`. It shows what it creates, updates and deletes;
   `esquadro gen -dry-run` shows the same with diffs, without writing.
{{- if uses "migrations"}}
3. Run `task migrate`: the change in the model became a new migration in
   `migrations/`, which is yours to review before applying.
{{- end}}

esquadro never overwrites a generated file you edited: it stops and tells
you. Write your own code in files of your own next to the generated ones;
`esquadro explain <file>` tells where a file came from.

## Layout

```
esquadro/            the model (.esq) and esquadro's state: edit the .esq files
cmd/                 the commands: {{if $http}}serve (the default), {{end}}{{if uses "migrations"}}migrate, {{end}}check
internal/<module>/   each module: its entities, rules and use cases
internal/platform/   what the modules share: config, logging{{if $http}}, the HTTP edge{{end}}{{if uses "postgres"}}, Postgres{{end}}
{{- if uses "migrations"}}
migrations/          the database migrations, one per change in the model
{{- end}}
{{- if uses "rest"}}
api/openapi.yaml     the API's contract
{{- end}}
```
{{- if .Modules}}

Modules:
{{range .Modules}}
- `internal/{{.Snake}}`: {{range $i, $e := .Entities}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}{{$e.Pascal}}{{end}}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

## Configuration

Everything has a value in development: the project runs as it is.
{{- if uses "env"}} The
environments are {{join ", " .Environments}}; `APP_ENV` picks one. Every variable
is documented in `.env.example`; each environment's values go in its
`.env.<environment>` file (out of git) or on its platform, and
`go run ./cmd/{{.App.Kebab}} env` shows where each value comes from, secrets hidden.
{{- else}} Environment variables change it in production: see
`internal/platform/config/config.go` for the whole list.
{{- end}}
